Topic 2 2.4 #2: Viruses and Worms (overview)

Virus: malware that attaches itself to a legitimate file or program and needs a person to run it before it can spread. It hides inside something normal and spreads when that file is opened or shared.
Analogy: like a cold passed on a shared cup. It only spreads when someone picks the cup up and uses it.

Messer covers a few virus types:

Program viruses: hide inside an application
Boot sector viruses: load when the computer starts, before the operating system
Script viruses: run in scripts, such as in a browser or the operating system
Macro viruses: hide in documents, like a Word or Excel file with macros
Fileless viruses: run only in memory, never saved to disk (linking back to memory injection)

Worm: malware that spreads by itself across a network, with no click or human action needed. It finds a vulnerable machine, infects it, then uses that machine to find the next one.
Analogy: an airborne infection spreading through a ward. Nobody has to touch anything, and it moves from bed to bed on its own.

Key exam difference: a virus needs a human to run it; a worm spreads by itself.
